; the built and natural objects that define the space where physics happens.
; not materials (what things are made of) — structural roles (what things do).
; a wall is a vertical barrier. a floor is a horizontal support below.
; a ground is the earth's surface. a ceiling is a horizontal barrier above.
; these are the boundary conditions of everyday physical reasoning.
; 
; structural objects have two roles in physics:
; 1. constraint: they define where things cannot go. the ball cannot pass the wall.
; 2. support:    they exert normal force. the floor pushes up on the ball.
; 
; gravity is always acting downward. structural objects resist it.
; things rest on floors because normal-force balances gravity.
; things fall until they hit a surface — then collision occurs.
; this is the default physics of everyday reality:
; gravity + surface = rest (if coefficient of restitution is low enough)
; gravity + surface = bounce (if coefficient of restitution is high)
; 
; in 3d / pratibimba: structural objects are static rigid bodies.
; they have collision meshes. they do not move. gravity acts on other things
; relative to them. they are the kshetra (field) in which the kshetrajna moves.

(kosha structures-varga
  (vishesa common-sense-varga)
  (swarupa subanta)
  (yukta normal-force constraint collision-varga domain-3d)
  (sthita gravity solid))
